  "emoji": "📰",
  "summary": "Review reading with real texts: find the main idea of a newspaper article from its headline, read a sign and a menu quickly, and guess new words from their root and the context, even when a text has no vowel marks.",
  "intro": "Today is reading review. Real Arabic texts, such as newspapers, magazines, signs and menus, are almost always printed without the vowel marks, الْحَرَكَات. In this course every word keeps them, so you can read with confidence, but good readers use other tools too. Read the headline, الْعُنْوَان, first; look for the main idea, الْفِكْرَة الرَّئِيسِيَّة, which is usually in the first paragraph; and skim for numbers and names. For a new word, find its root, جَذْر, and look at the context, السِّيَاق: مَلْعَب has the root l-ʿ-b (play), and the pattern ma-…-a- often means a place, so it is a playground. Headlines are full of iḍāfa, like عُنْوَانُ الْمَقَالَةِ (the headline of the article), and الَّذِي and الَّتِي help you follow long sentences. When you read a headline aloud, stop on the bare word: مَجَلَّة is said majalla.",
  "objectives": [
    "Find the main idea of a short newspaper article from its headline and first paragraph",
    "Read a sign and a menu quickly by looking only for what you need",
    "Guess a new word from its root and its context, like مَلْعَب (playground)",
    "Use iḍāfa like عُنْوَانُ الْمَقَالَةِ and the relative words الَّذِي and الَّتِي when you talk about a text"
  ],
