    "culture": "Almost everything adults read in Arabic, from newspapers and novels to street signs, menus and text messages, is written without vowel marks. Readers know the words and use the context, so كُتُب (books) and كَتَبَ (he wrote) look the same on paper and are still easy to tell apart. The marks appear in the Qur'an, in poetry, in children's books and in books for learners, and sometimes on a single word to avoid a misunderstanding.",
